<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tamils</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tamil people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tamils are a people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> whose language, Tamil, has a literary tradition of more than two thousand years. Most live in the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> and in northern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, where a Tamil kingdom ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and a separate community descended from 19th-century plantation workers lives in Sri Lanka's central highlands. Large diasporas live in Malaysia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and elsewhere.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tamils.jpg" alt="Tamils" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hishampgm · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pongal_Festival.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tamils are a Dravidian ethnic group who natively speak Tamil and trace their ancestry mainly to the south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. Archaeological evidence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> indicates continuous human occupation for more than 3,800 years. Megalithic urn burials at Adichanallur date back to 1500 BCE, and excavations at Keezhadi have revealed a large urban settlement, with its earliest artefact dated to 580 BCE. Inscriptions and potsherds from these sites use Tamil Brahmi, a rudimentary script dated to the 6th century BCE. The meaning of the name Tamil is unclear, and scholars have proposed several derivations, among them 'self-speak' or 'our own speech'.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Sangam period, between about 300 BCE and 300 CE, the Tamil country, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamilakam">Tamilakam</a>, was ruled by the three crowned kings, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>, with smaller Velir chieftains beside them. Coastal towns grew with trade across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, and excavated artefacts indicate early trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. The Pallavas later ruled from the 3rd to the 9th century CE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> from the 14th to the 17th century.</p><p>In the 10th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> established their influence across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and scripts carried by Tamil traders, such as Grantha and Pallava, shaped the development of many Southeast Asian scripts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna-kingdom">Jaffna Kingdom</a> controlled the Tamil territory in the north of Sri Lanka from the 13th to the 17th century. European colonisation began in the 17th century.</p><p>Tamils have migrated worldwide since the 19th century. Many went as indentured labourers or traders to British territories such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, and a large emigration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lankan-tamils">Sri Lankan Tamils</a> began in the 1980s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In India Tamils form about 5.7 per cent of the population and the majority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> and Puducherry, and they are a significant share of the populations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, Malaysia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>. Sri Lanka's government counts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lankan-tamils">Sri Lankan Tamils</a>, descended from the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna-kingdom">Jaffna Kingdom</a> and the eastern Vannimai chiefdoms, separately from Indian Origin Tamils, descendants of 19th-century plantation labourers in the central highlands.</p><p>Tamil literature has an antiquity of more than two thousand years, beginning with the Sangam anthologies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> is the major religion, with smaller numbers of Christians and Muslims. Pongal, a harvest festival in January, and Puthandu, the new year in April, are major festivals, and Bharatanatyam is among the classical performing arts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tamils” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The state's politics also fed the language movements of independent India: <b>Tamils</b> in the southern taluks founded the All Travancore Tamilian Congress at Nagercoil on 16 December 1945, renamed the Travancore Tamil Nadu Congress in 1946, which under A. Nesamony won 14 seats in the first assembly; the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/travancore">Travancore · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Tamil is a Dravidian language attested since about 300 BCE, spoken by the <b>Tamil people</b> of southern India and Sri Lanka and by communities across the world.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Tolkappiyam, Mayon, “the dark one”, identified with Vishnu, is the deity of the pastoral mullai land, and Ceyon, “the red one”, is Murugan, praised in the poems as “the favoured god of the <b>Tamils</b>”, ever young and seated on a blue peacock.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The new state disenfranchised most plantation <b>Tamils</b> of Indian origin through the citizenship laws of 1948 and 1949.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-ceylon">Dominion of Ceylon</a></li></ul></section>
